-- title:
--   Inertia at q with q² ‖ M: principal series versus supercuspidal
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a positive integer and let $g$ be a weight-two cusp form for $\Gamma_0(M)$ which is a newform, that is, a normalised eigenform (its $q$-expansion coefficients satisfy $a_1=1$, multiplicativity at coprime arguments and the usual recursions at prime powers) whose eigensystem is not matched, away from $M$, by a normalised eigenform of any proper divisor level. Let $\lambda$ be a prime with $\lambda\neq 2$, let $S$ be a finite set of natural numbers, and let $O'$ be a complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero which is a domain with finite residue field and with $\lambda$ in its maximal ideal. Let $\chi_g$ be a ring homomorphism to $\mathbb{C}$ from the level-$M$ weight-two Hecke algebra, the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb{C}}$ of the cusp forms generated by the operators $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell\nmid M$, $\ell\notin S$ and $U_p$ for primes $p\mid M$, $p\notin S$, with $\chi_g(T_\ell)$ the $\ell$-th coefficient of $g$ for every such $\ell$; let $\iota$ be a ring homomorphism from the image of $\chi_g$ into $O'$. Let $\rho$ be a $\lambda$-adic representation over $O'$, i.e. a monoid homomorphism from $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ to the endomorphisms of a free $O'$-module of rank $2$, continuous for the maximal-ideal-adic filtration, such that its residual representation on the residue-field base change has no invariant submodule other than $\bot$ and $\top$ and sends every involution $\neq 1$ to an endomorphism of determinant $-1$, and such that for every prime $\ell\nmid M$ with $\ell\notin S$, every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\ell$ in its nonunits and every $\sigma$ which is a Frobenius at $\ell$ for $A$, the characteristic polynomial of $\rho(\sigma)$ is $X^2-\iota(\chi_g(T_\ell))X+\ell$. Let $q$ be a prime with $q\neq\lambda$ and with $M.\mathrm{factorization}\,q=2$, i.e. $q^2\Vert M$. Then there are a local domain $O''$, an injective local ring homomorphism $j:O'\to O''$, and a function $a$ from $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ to $(O'')^\times$ such that: (1) for every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $q$ and every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $P$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, the image under $j$ of the characteristic polynomial of $\rho(\sigma)$ equals $(X-a\sigma)(X-(a\sigma)^{-1})$; (2) for every nonzero function $\Phi$ on $\mathrm{GL}_2$ of the adeles of $\mathbb{Q}$ which is an adelic lift of $g$ (left invariant under the global points, right invariant under the finite level-one subgroup attached to the ideal $(M)$, and agreeing with $g$ at $i$ after the archimedean slash action), for all characters $\mu_1,\mu_2:\mathbb{Q}_q^\times\to\mathbb{C}^\times$ and every nonzero $\mathbb{C}$-linear $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_q)$-equivariant map $f$ from the span of the translates of $\Phi$ to the principal series space for $\mu_1,\mu_2$, the order of $a\sigma$ is matched with the order of $\mu_1$ on norm-one units in both directions: every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of every $P$ over $q$ admits a unit $u$ of $\mathbb{Q}_q$ of absolute value $1$ with $(a\sigma)^n=1\iff\mu_1(u)^n=1$ for all $n$, and conversely every such $u$ and every such $P$ admit a $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup with the same equivalence; and (3) granting again that $M.\mathrm{factorization}\,q=2$, which is restated as an explicit antecedent, if $\Phi$ is a nonzero adelic lift of $g$ for which every equivariant linear map into every such principal series space vanishes, then for every valuation subring $P$ over $q$ there is a $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $P$ with $(a\sigma)^{q-1}\neq 1$.
--
--   This is the form of local–global compatibility at a prime $q$ with $q^2\Vert M$, read on inertia: the eigenvalues of $\rho$ on inertia at $q$ are recorded by a single unit-valued function $a$ with $a\sigma$ and $(a\sigma)^{-1}$ as roots, and the order of $a\sigma$ is tied either to the restriction of the principal-series character $\mu_1$ to the units (when an equivariant map into a principal series exists) or to non-divisibility by $q-1$ (in the supercuspidal case). It is used to produce a $\lambda$-adic representation attached to $g$ whose restriction to inertia at $q$ is not unipotent.
